def P(num,h,fig,tier,grant,inv,title,teaches,figs,status="Expired"):
    return dict(num=num,hash=h,fig=fig,tier=tier,grant=grant,inv=inv,title=title,teaches=teaches,figs=figs,status=status)
INTRO = "<p>Lifting, cleaning and distilling water were among the busiest fields of 19th- and early-20th-century invention. These patents show how real pumps, rams, filters and stills were built &mdash; from a wooden chain pump to a municipal rapid sand filter. Use them with the wells, pumps, filtering and disinfection chapters of this guide, and still disinfect filtered water before drinking.</p>"
UNPATENTED = ["The <b>Archimedes screw</b>, the <b>shaduf</b> (counterweighted bucket pole) and the <b>windlass-and-bucket well</b> are ancient and free to copy.",
 "The <b>hydraulic ram</b> itself dates from the 1790s in Europe; the US patents below are later improvements to its valves and air chamber."]
PATENTS = [
P("US29466","81/8a/68/b118889a349be9/US29466.pdf","ee/01/7f/0d769d77a66935/US29466-drawings-page-1.png","A","1860-08-07","Amos Coates","Chain-pump",
  "A chain pump for a well: an endless chain of discs or buckets is cranked up through a pipe, each disc lifting the water above it to a spout in the pump box. Simple wood and iron, very tolerant of dirty water.","Fig. 1 perspective of the pump box; Fig. 2 section of box and well showing chain and pipe"),
P("US895296","7b/30/6d/514dbe59e6c0c5/US895296.pdf","6e/a8/e8/6fe4080b86dc54/US895296-drawings-page-1.png","A","1908-08-04","Johan M. K. Pennink","Sand filter",
  "Written by the director of Amsterdam's waterworks: a sand filter only cleans water well (biologically) once a fine layer of organic matter builds up on top of the sand. The patent covers protecting and managing that top layer &mdash; the same 'biolayer' that makes a home slow sand or biosand filter work.","Filter bed section with water inlet, sand layers and outlet"),
P("US983424","d3/15/5a/dd5fa96f3b5984/US983424.pdf","b0/4b/3d/5cb98a55f004f0/US983424-drawings-page-1.png","A","1911-02-07","Albert M. Brosius","Solar still",
  "A still heated by the sun: water is spread in a thin current over a sun-heated surface so it evaporates, and the vapour is condensed and collected as clean water. Removes salt and germs with no fuel.","Section of the still showing the heated surface, cover and collecting channel"),
P("US936244","06/50/a2/ccae274bf22cde/US936244.pdf","ab/98/e6/4e965992c58dd6/US936244-drawings-page-1.png","B","1909-10-05","Frederick Hesse","Hydraulic ram",
  "A water-powered pump with no motor: water rushing down a drive pipe slams the waste valve shut, and the pressure surge pushes a little water past a check valve into an air chamber and up a delivery pipe. Runs day and night on a small stream with a few feet of fall.","Section of the ram body: waste valve, air chamber above it, delivery outlet"),
P("US577058","65/3c/e1/1ecdbc2dbad5a4/US577058.pdf","64/07/40/3484c7b79d72c6/US577058-drawings-page-1.png","B","1897-02-16","Luther Myers","Force-pump for wells or cisterns",
  "A hand force pump: a piston and check valves in a cylinder lift water from a well or cistern and can push it up to a tank or hose, unlike a simple lift pump. Shows the valve and cylinder layout a workshop can cast or machine.","Sectional view of cylinder, piston, valves and handle linkage"),
P("US2427262","21/49/87/9bd128d3e39c8d/US2427262.pdf","dd/86/b6/f4f349ae125b0b/US2427262-drawings-page-1.png","B","1947-09-09","William R. P. Delano","Inflatable solar still",
  "A WWII-era inflatable still for distilling sea water in a lifeboat: a transparent envelope over a dark wick traps sun heat, and fresh water condenses on the cover and drains to a collector. The same idea as a plastic-sheet ground still.","Cutaway of envelope, wick, condensing surface and fresh-water outlet"),
P("US273542","2d/89/18/0fe0b8a4f241cb/US273542.pdf","pdf:1","C","1883-03-06","John W. Hyatt","Filter",
  "An early large-volume mechanical filter for town water: water is forced through a sand bed in a closed vessel, with built-in means of cleaning the filter bed without digging it out. Foundation of the 'American' pressure-filter industry.","Filter vessel section, sand bed and cleaning apparatus"),
P("US1248127","b1/a7/55/a8c8673c327587/US1248127.pdf","5e/03/e7/3f8c0b2c1f4579/US1248127-drawings-page-1.png","C","1917-11-27","William M. Jewell","Art of filtration",
  "The rapid sand filter cleaned by reversing the flow (backwashing): instead of scraping the sand, water is driven up through the bed to wash out trapped dirt, saving labour and water. The method still used in every town water plant.","Filter tank section with under-drains, wash troughs and valve piping"),
P("US571776","44/a8/60/92ee3f4cf535b2/US571776.pdf","a5/7b/30/01107a22fd8cd9/US571776-drawings-page-1.png","A","1896-11-24","Nathan H. Long","Rain-water filter",
  "A sheet-metal box that hangs between the downspout and the cistern. A removable 'shoe' with screened, curved sections lets water drop through while leaves and dirt washed off the roof slide off and out. A dam and a hinged flap check the flow in a heavy downpour. The patent says it needs no filtering material and cleans itself; treat cistern water before drinking all the same.","Fig. 1 perspective of the filter on the spout; Fig. 2 section through the screen shoe, dam and flap"),
P("US679169","c9/a2/0f/09d9ef905aa7df/US679169.pdf","02/0c/ad/f725e4cac88052/US679169-drawings-page-1.png","B","1901-07-23","Arthur E. Hunt","Driven-well filter",
  "A sand filter that sits between a driven well and its pump. Water comes up the well pipe into a cast chamber and passes a gauze-covered strainer, so the sand settles out before it reaches the pump's valves. A pitcher pump can bolt straight on top, or a deep-well cylinder can be coupled below.","Fig. 1 filter with pitcher pump; Fig. 3 section of the chamber; Fig. 4 the gauze strainer"),
P("US1007647","e4/a2/10/adc8e7963a3789/US1007647.pdf","3d/bd/8b/a6603baae9129d/US1007647-drawings-page-1.png","C","1911-10-31","Carl R. Darnall","Process of purifying water and sewage",
  "Disinfecting large volumes of water with dry chlorine gas, metered from a steel cylinder of liquefied chlorine with a pressure gauge into a mixing tank or a water main. Darnall chose gas because the chlorine content of chlorinated lime and soda varies so much, while gas can be metered accurately. This is water-works equipment, and chlorine gas is highly toxic. At home, use household bleach at the doses in this guide.","Fig. 1 cylinder, gauge and mixing tank; Figs. 8-9 feeding gas into a conduit"),
]
